00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.630
Now, the stigma surrounding therapy

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.180
and seeking help for mental health issues

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.690
has dissolved significantly
over recent years.

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:22.930
This is unequivocally great news.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:23.920
Especially when you consider

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.820
how mental health used to be
treated in the good 'ol days,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.490
which definitely were old,
but definitely were not good.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:31.770
Early psychiatric treatment

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.390
typically involved confining
patients to lunatic asylums

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:37.370
with horrible living conditions.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.680
Often, patients were sterilized.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.260
Women could be kept in asylums

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.170
purely on their husbands' recommendations.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.670
- Why would your husband do that?

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.670
- He said something about me participating

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.500
in some flag burning at a
local suffragette protest.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.810
He was not happy with my decisions

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:53.618
and found me generally annoying.

00:02:53.618 --> 00:02:56.010
- LGBTQ+ folks and other
marginalized people

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.540
were also often considered pathological.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.910
And typically, super-churchy people

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:05.380
assumed all these issues
stemmed from demonic possession.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:06.615
So...

00:03:06.615 --> 00:03:08.370
(Michael sighs) It's not good.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:11.250
In short, psychiatry was
more about punishing folks

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.120
who failed to conform to social norms,

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.070
with a heavy dose of sexism,

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.740
homophobia, racism, and eugenics.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.570
Then, in 1895, everybody's
favorite goatee guy,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.820
Sigmund Freud, teamed up with Josef Breuer

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.170
to publish "Studies in Hysteria,"

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:28.260
which championed the talking cure

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.230
to help work through psychological trauma.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.260
You know the one: a
patient lies on the couch

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.030
and talks at length to their analyst

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:36.450
about all their deepest
thoughts and feelings.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.300
Importantly, Freudian psychology

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:41.140
was less about offering practical
fixes for your problems,

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.930
and more about understanding
your negative thought patterns,

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.310
neuroses, and so on.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.760
His goal was to help "make
the unconscious conscious,"

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.580
which could eventually help
improve people's mental health.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.260
Psychotherapy became
increasingly popular in the US

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:57.400
around World War II and
then boomed in the 1950s,

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.580
in what scholar Daniel P. Kamienski calls

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.210
the "Age of Affluence and Anxiety."

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.190
This was due to the intersecting phenomena

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:06.070
of the post-war economic boom,

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:09.380
with the high numbers of World
War II soldiers with PTSD,

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.260
then referred to as shell shock,

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:14.000
as well as widespread fear
about nuclear weapons.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.350
As Kamienski notes, this era of therapy

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:20.010
saw the origin of an emphasis
on patients doing the work.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:23.330
As he puts it, "Psychoanalysts
advised patients

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:25.860
that the traditional
American value of hard work

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:27.750
needed to be applied to therapy

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:30.420
as well as to their personal
pursuit of happiness."

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.470
This was a big change from Freud

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.850
having you articulate traumatic memories

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:37.570
while half-asleep on a tiny couch.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:40.960
At the same time,
psychology-infused self-help books

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.140
boomed in popularity,

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.330
and they were usually about

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:45.350
pulling yourself up by your bootstraps

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.610
and tackling your maladaptive
emotional responses.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.550
Positive psychologists pushed
the idea of self-improvement

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:53.720
and reaching your full potential.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.000
If it sounds kinda like
motivational speaking,

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.180
that's because it basically was.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:01.000
But what was really the
purpose of psychoanalysis?

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.410
According to Kamienski,

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.410
therapy functioned largely as a way

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:06.330
to lead people into conformity.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:08.440
In particular, conformity with America's

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.110
booming consumer economy and workforce.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:14.300
As he writes, "By
advocating clearly defined,

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.960
traditional gender roles
and normative sexuality

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:18.560
set in the single-family,

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.720
psychoanalysts helped legitimize

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.690
and popularize The American Dream."

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:23.980
Good mental health, then,

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:27.210
was largely defined as the
ability to fit societal standards

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.080
surrounding work, gender,
family, and so on.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:32.380
One famous psychoanalysis institute,

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.850
The Menninger Clinic,
even explicitly promised

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:38.130
to return its patients to
the useful citizenship.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.740
Kamienski writes that,
"Psychoanalysts promised

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.750
that Americans in therapy
could be made to conform,

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:45.360
with a high degree of efficiency,

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.040
to the values of hard work
and productivity in work,

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:49.460
and in their personal lives.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:53.170
In doing so, they reflected
and reinforced industriousness

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:55.540
as a key value of Americans."

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:56.750
So, what we have here

00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:00.490
are three important aspects
of 1950s-style therapy:

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.760
One, an emphasis on the
patient's hard work;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.270
two, conformity to American values;

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:09.840
and three, becoming a
productive member of society.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.160
This would only grow more
true in the '60s and '70s,

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:16.410
as cognitive behavioral therapy, or CBT,

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.300
became all the rage.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.420
CBT focuses on recognizing

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.690
and rationalizing
negative thought patterns.

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:25.090
For example, if your brain is telling you

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:27.910
that there's a creepy
clown hiding in your car,

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:30.270
a CBT therapist would ask
you to think critically

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:33.360
about the statistical
likelihood of this being so.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.377
They'd suggest alternative thoughts, like,

00:06:35.377 --> 00:06:38.220
"It's very unlikely that
there is a creepy clown

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.690
hiding in my car."

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.180
This can be especially useful
after doing Uber pick-ups

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.000
the weekend of The Clown Convention.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.820
Indeed, CBT encourages you

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:47.660
to think critically about
your thought patterns

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:49.040
in order to change them.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.690
It teaches logical skills

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:53.130
to deal with distressing
situations and emotions,

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.670
with the ultimate goal of
significantly mitigating

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:59.120
or even totally eliminating
their impact on your life.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:01.650
As journalist Oliver Burkeman explains,

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.987
CBT is based on the principle that,

00:07:03.987 --> 00:07:07.230
"Your distress is caused
by your irrational beliefs,

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:08.510
and it's within your power

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:11.150
to seize hold of those
beliefs and change them."

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:15.010
Again, we see emphasis on the
individual doing the work,

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:16.470
with the help of a therapist,

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:18.660
to ultimately overcome their issues.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:20.610
This contrasts against the Freudian method

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:22.300
of understanding unconscious drives

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:25.270
in order to release repressed
experiences and emotions.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:27.070
Notably, while Freud's methods

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:29.170
were expected to take many years,

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:32.090
CBT is considered a short-term method,

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:33.980
at least by insurance companies,

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:36.630
typically consisting
of five to 20 sessions.

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.990
And to be clear, it's not the
individual therapist's fault

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:41.040
that you're only allowed

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:43.190
to have ten sessions a year or whatever.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:45.060
They're just playing by the rules set

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:46.850
by private insurance companies.

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:50.600
And no doubt, CBT can
be enormously helpful

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:53.220
for things like phobias
and sleep disorders.

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:56.620
But it's been critiqued for
offering cheap, fast solutions,

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:58.410
with some dissenting therapists arguing

00:07:58.410 --> 00:08:01.710
that it pushes a think
yourself to wellness narrative.

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:05.160
CBT undoubtedly has changed tons of lives,

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:07.270
again, yours truly.

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:09.610
Still, there's a growing pile of research

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:12.920
suggesting that it's not a
one-size-fits-all solution,

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:15.340
particularly when it comes to depression.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:17.770
So we started with a long-term commitment

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:20.560
based on a good relationship
between therapist and patient,

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.840
centered around exploring the unconscious.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:26.050
And we're trending toward
therapy as a self-led,

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:27.710
bite-sized course.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:29.510
And if you don't have the time, insurance,

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:31.330
or resources to afford that?

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:33.780
Well, now Silicon Valley has an answer:

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:36.170
therapy, but, like, make it an app.

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:37.740
You've likely already seen them:

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:40.530
meditation apps, breathing exercise apps,

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.010
mood trackers, and self-led therapy apps

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:45.120
for just about any mental health issues.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.520
We're also offered mindfulness
apps like Headspace,

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:49.350
which promises to reduce your stress

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.230
and improve your sleep
in just 3 minutes a day.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:53.300
It's kind of like therapy

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:55.020
for people without the time or money

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:58.134
to be emotionally unzipped by
a professional twice a month.

00:08:58.134 --> 00:08:59.513
(Frank screams)

00:08:59.513 --> 00:09:00.800
- You unzipped me!

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:02.730
- And undoubtedly, these platforms

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.650
have made mental health
care a lot more accessible,

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:06.830
which is great.

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:09.430
And online therapy has been
proven to have real benefits

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:11.230
for people struggling with depression.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:14.300
Text-based therapy also adds
an element of anonymity,

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:15.470
which can be great for people

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:17.880
who struggle to talk
about their mental health.

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:20.080
What's more, subscriptions
for mental health apps

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:21.820
can save people tons of money

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:23.600
compared to conventional therapy,

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.490
which makes high-end gym
memberships look cheap.

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:28.100
This can be a life-saver,

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:29.950
especially when we consider that poverty

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:32.550
is a major fact in anxiety, depression,

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:33.950
and substance-abuse issues

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:36.070
and that lack of access
to mental health services

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:39.070
is the root of the mental
health crisis in the US.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:43.120
Importantly, though, many
of these apps rely on CBT,

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:44.680
boasting that their use of the model

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:47.910
will help you stop feeling
anxious, depressed, stressed,

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:49.300
or unable to sleep.

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:51.230
And the marketing of CBT on these apps

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:53.950
exacerbates the
one-size-fits-all mentality

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:56.370
that people critique the
method for in the first place,

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:58.670
turning something as
multifaceted and personal

00:09:58.670 --> 00:09:59.840
as mental health care

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:02.860
into Uber, but for your unconscious mind.

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:04.500
This raises an important question.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:06.070
By pushing convenient apps

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:07.570
to help manage mental health issues

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:11.200
rather than increasing access
to and funding for therapy,

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.650
are we, as a society,
putting too much pressure

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:15.620
on people struggling
with mental health issues

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:17.580
to understand themselves

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.000
and guide themselves
through their own recovery?

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.960
With apps, we've seen the
idea of doing the work

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:26.250
increasingly become about
doing the work alone.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:29.790
With apps, the onus is often
entirely on the individual

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:32.980
to actively engage with and
work through their problems.

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:36.390
Fittingly, the apps are also
tailor-made for a gig economy,

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:38.540
when everybody is working multiple jobs

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:40.110
with no time to fully commit

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:41.830
to working on their mental health.

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:44.900
But hey, at least you can dip
into five minutes of therapy

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:47.570
in between DoorDash deliveries, right?

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:50.450
This only exacerbates
another existing problem

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:52.750
in cognitive behavioral therapy:

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:55.440
its historic failure to
address the systemic issues

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:58.610
that often contribute to
individual mental health issues.

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:01.500
You might, say, be taught
how to manage the stress

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:02.680
of living in poverty

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:05.750
or anxieties about the unstable
job market and economy.

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:07.500
But being able to manage these

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:09.660
doesn't change these circumstances.

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:11.460
You're still facing down the possibilities

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:15.110
of not being able to afford
dinner or find a new job.

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:18.690
Now, you've simply learned
not to be as worried about it,

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:19.970
and thus are able to be

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:22.290
a higher-functioning member of society.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.150
Workplace counseling and support options

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.550
illustrate the tension
between solving individual

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:28.810
versus systemic problems perfectly.

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:31.920
Employee Assistance Programs, or EAPs,

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:34.360
began in the late '30s and early '40s

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:37.260
to help identify workers whose
struggles with alcoholism

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:38.730
were making them less productive.

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:40.240
If you're struggling at work,

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:42.550
whether due to depression or stress,

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:44.010
you might be offered counseling,

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.760
which often comes with
homework and self-led regimens

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:48.830
to help you overcome your problems.

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:52.130
What appears to be an
employer's genuine act of care

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:54.290
could cynically be seen as a situation

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:55.990
where interests collide:

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:57.740
your interest in feeling better

00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:59.440
meets your employer's interest

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:02.400
in seeing you meet your
productivity potential at work.

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.600
Feel the 1950's useful citizen vibes here?

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:07.650
Importantly, EAPs typically focus

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:10.500
on the few most obviously
struggling workers.

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:13.497
As scholars J. Berridge
and C. L. Cooper write,

00:12:13.497 --> 00:12:15.890
"The concept of the troubled employee

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:18.030
is at the heart of the EAP."

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:19.640
This approach can be problematic

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:21.010
because it doesn't consider

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:25.110
where those troubled employees'
troubles actually come from,

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:27.470
which might be the workplace itself.

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:30.150
This certainly seems true in
cases like that of Google,

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:33.020
which offered counseling to
victims of workplace misconduct,

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:36.050
allegedly without bothering
to reexamine the environment

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:37.930
that fostered that misconduct.

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:40.407
As Michael P. Leiter and
J. Michael Wahlan note,

00:12:40.407 --> 00:12:43.400
"Even when effective,
individually-focused interventions

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:45.180
may do little more than enable people

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:48.430
to tolerate poorly-managed situations."

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:50.930
And beyond the microcosm of the workplace,

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:52.980
there's no shortage of systemic problems

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:54.350
to be found these days.

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:57.310
Our current economic realities,
like the decline of unions,

00:12:57.310 --> 00:12:58.940
or rising wealth inequality,

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:01.110
have even been explicitly linked

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:02.740
to rising mental health issues.

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:04.500
Loneliness is also on the rise,

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:06.270
as Americans are working longer hours

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:07.820
with fewer support systems.

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:09.730
This all leaves people more reliant

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:11.290
on mental health professionals.

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:13.590
That is, when they can
afford the time and cost

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:14.690
of seeing them.

00:13:14.690 --> 00:13:17.630
On top of that, job insecurity
due to the gig economy,

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:19.110
lack of long-term contracts,

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:21.080
and fewer opportunities for promotion

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:22.430
can also contribute to your risk

00:13:22.430 --> 00:13:24.180
of developing mental health issues.

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:26.680
Systemic economic issues
like childhood poverty

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:28.580
can also have the same effect.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:30.840
But it's not just economic issues

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:32.730
that negatively impact mental health;

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:34.640
racism and mass incarceration

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:37.980
have had a staggering impact
on marginalized communities.

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:39.730
Suicide rates among young Black people

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:44.380
have increased by 73%
between 1991 and 2017,

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:46.380
while accessing treatment
in these communities

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:49.160
remains more difficult
than for their White peers.

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:50.960
And despite its success

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:52.820
in treating people with anxiety disorders

00:13:52.820 --> 00:13:54.140
and other mental health issues,

00:13:54.140 --> 00:13:57.100
modern CBT therapy still has flaws

00:13:57.100 --> 00:13:59.640
when it comes to working
with marginalized people.

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:03.030
Remember, it's a highly
individualistic therapy,

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:04.630
which hinges on the assumption

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:07.730
that a patient has faulty
or irrational thoughts.

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:09.020
This framing makes it easy

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:11.560
for systemic issues to go unaddressed.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:14.700
For example, it's reasonable
that systemic racism,

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:16.950
sexism, or homophobia in America

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:19.030
can beget anxiety or depression.

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:20.690
You can't be expected

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:23.110
to positive-think your
way out of that reality.

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:24.480
Psychiatrists are still working

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:27.650
on increasing the cultural
sensitivity of CBT

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:29.810
so that marginalized groups are validated

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:31.100
while they receive treatment,

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:33.130
rather than encouraged to view anxieties

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:36.380
formed around their lived
experience as irrational.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:39.680
And lest it sound like we're
criticizing therapists,

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:41.487
let us quote scholar James Hillman,

00:14:41.487 --> 00:14:43.240
"When I criticize therapy,

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:45.070
I'm not really out to get therapists.

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:45.903
I think they're doing

00:14:45.903 --> 00:14:48.190
some of the most important
work in the culture

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:50.770
because they are sincerely
trying to pick up the pieces

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:53.150
that capitalism throws into the street."

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:56.130
Indeed, therapy is a difficult profession,

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:57.060
and getting it right

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:58.810
when you have people's
mental health in your hands

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:00.150
can be taxing.

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:02.970
What's more, therapists
can't necessarily help

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:05.010
when it comes to systemic issues;

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:08.100
they can only support their
patients within an unjust world

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:10.500
and address the impact it has on them.

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:12.990
So, where do we go from here?

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:14.650
Scholar Micah Ingle argues

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:16.820
that as long as people consider
their mental health issues

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:20.270
by a purely medical or
cognitive behavioral model,

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:22.740
the responsibility will
remain on each individual

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:26.190
to get better without
inspiring systemic change.

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:28.170
But before you sink into despair,

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:29.550
know that Ingle has some thoughts

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:31.610
on what therapy could look like.

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:34.850
One interesting alternative he
offers is narrative therapy,

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:36.550
which helps people understand

00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:39.310
and deconstruct the way
they see themselves,

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:41.940
and why they see themselves in that way.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:44.520
Therapists and patients
reauthor their lives

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:47.150
to retrace where their
self-perception comes from,

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:49.280
and address the social
and personal factors

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:50.730
that have impacted them.

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:52.210
This can mitigate the effects

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:53.890
of systemic problems on patients

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:55.160
by addressing the patient

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:57.300
in the context of the system itself.

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:00.250
Will this, or any other
new forms of therapy

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:01.760
take off in our lifetimes?

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:03.430
It remains to be seen.

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:05.820
In the meantime, we're stuck with a world

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:07.950
that exposes us to injustice and trauma,

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:10.680
and healthcare providers who
charge us hundreds of dollars

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:12.640
to even try to feel better about it,

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:14.720
while sometimes neglecting
the broader reasons

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:16.250
for our mental health issues.

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:17.570
But hey, at least you know

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:19.340
there's no creepy clown in your car.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:20.430
I mean, unless...

00:16:20.430 --> 00:16:22.920
you've been the clown all along.

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:24.240
But what do you-all think?

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:26.700
Is modern therapy asking
too much of its patients?

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:27.870
Have mental health apps

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:30.010
revolutionized mental health support,

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:31.620
or merely commodified it?

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:33.350
Let us know in the comments.

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:35.340
Big thanks to our patrons
for all your support,

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:37.870
and don't forget to
listen to our podcasts.

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:39.490
Hyperfixate on that subscribe button

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:41.610
like it's your childhood
abandonment issues

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:43.280
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:45.130
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:45.963
Later.

00:16:45.963 --> 00:16:49.420
(upbeat electronic music)

